# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Vault .gitignore
#
# Principle: git tracks TEXT (notes, configs, scripts).
# Binaries (PDFs, images) stay in Google Drive and are captured
# by the periodic zip snapshots — see 90-System/scripts/vault_snapshot.sh
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── OS / Python cruft ──
.DS_Store
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/

# ── Obsidian: keep settings, drop volatile state and vendored plugin code ──
# workspace.json is tracked ONCE as the seed layout (sidebars, ribbon, calendar
# pane). Obsidian rewrites it on every pane move; after your first open, stop
# git from seeing that churn with:
#   git update-index --skip-worktree .obsidian/workspace.json
.obsidian/workspace-mobile.json
# Plugin code is redownloadable from the community store, so only settings
# (data.json) are tracked. manifest.json is deliberately NOT tracked: a folder
# holding a manifest but no main.js makes Obsidian think the plugin is installed
# and refuse to reinstall it. Without the manifest, the community browser offers
# Install and writes the code in next to the existing settings.
.obsidian/plugins/*/main.js
.obsidian/plugins/*/styles.css
.obsidian/plugins/*/manifest.json
.obsidian/themes/
.trash/

# ── Binaries: Drive + zip snapshots, not git ──
90-System/attachments/*
90-System/images/*
!90-System/attachments/README.md
!90-System/images/README.md

# ── Plugin state that holds credentials or per-account URLs ──
# Granola stores OAuth tokens in its data.json; Tasknotes stores your private
# calendar ICS URL. Keep both out of any repo that leaves your machine. The
# scrubbed data.example.json files ARE tracked — copy them into place on setup.
.obsidian/plugins/granola-meetings-simple-sync/data.json
.obsidian/plugins/tasknotes/data.json

# ── Claude Code: track shared config, ignore machine-local state ──
.claude/settings.local.json

# ── Scratch / external tool state ──
.codex-google-docs/
outputs/
